// queries.memql -- the __PRODUCT__ product pack (starter). Pure DSL, no Go.
//
// Two engine-executed reads over the pack's own greeting concept:
//   - myGreetings: the OWNED client read. The row set is gated by
//     ownerUserId==actor.userId server-side, so a caller only ever sees their
//     own greetings and no arg widens that set. The client + the ListGreetings
//     tool call this one.
//   - greetingsForOwner: an owner-parameterized read used by the
//     greetOnGreetingCreate automation, which runs under a SYSTEM actor (so
//     actor.userId is not the greeting's owner -- it must pass the owner id from
//     the event payload). NOT surfaced as a tool (it takes an explicit owner),
//     and marked `@serverOnly` because of it: the filter selects by a
//     user-scope column from a caller-supplied argument, which is precisely
//     the shape a client must never be able to aim. `@serverOnly` bars
//     client-originated calls at EXECUTION against auth.CallOrigin (engine
//     memql#2800) rather than merely hiding the construct, and it is the
//     annotation the engine's own per-row authz contract gate names for "an
//     automation acting on a user other than the actor" -- without it the
//     query fails that gate at load (`contract-gate:authz-user-scope`), which
//     refuses boot under strict-boot and fails `memqllint`. A tree-loaded
//     automation reaches it because the engine stamps the internal call
//     origin for those; a caller-submitted bundle does not.
// Reading the auth envelope is a DECLARED capability: a construct whose body
// touches actor.* carries a bare `@actor` annotation (engine memql#2621), and
// the loader rejects it otherwise. Row intrinsics in sort keys take the `row.`
// namespace (`row.createdAt`, memql#2786); payload fields stay bare.
// Grow this file with the product's own queries.

use __PRODUCT__.concepts.{ greeting }

@enabled
@description("List the caller's greetings, newest first. Owned: the row set is gated by ownerUserId==actor.userId server-side, so cross-user reads are impossible.")
@actor
query greeting myGreetings {
  filter   ownerUserId==actor.userId
  sort     "row.createdAt", "desc"
  paginate 50
  shape    greetingFull
}

@enabled
@description("List the greetings owned by a given user, newest first. Used by the greetOnGreetingCreate automation, which runs under a system actor and passes the owner id from event.payload.ownerUserId rather than relying on actor.userId. Not surfaced as a tool (it takes an explicit owner arg).")
@serverOnly
query greeting greetingsForOwner {
  args {
    ownerUserId  string  @required
  }
  filter   ownerUserId==args.ownerUserId
  sort     "row.createdAt", "desc"
  paginate 50
  shape    greetingFull
}
